{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC rate-limit headers are not honoured; a 429 is a hard failure\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nJira Data Center has built-in rate limiting (Administration → System → Rate limiting), introduced in\n**8.6**, **Data Center only**, and **OFF BY DEFAULT** — an administrator enables it. It is a per-user\nTOKEN BUCKET with a fill rate, not a fixed window.\n\nrebar's DC transport does not handle it at all. `_with_connection_retry` retries only\nconnection-level faults and its docstring states explicitly that `jira.exceptions.JIRAError` — \"any\nHTTP 4xx/5xx response\" — is NOT in the retry set. A 429 therefore propagates as a hard failure on the\nfirst occurrence, aborting whatever mutation or read was in flight, on an instance that was merely\nasking rebar to slow down.\n\nTwo constraints follow, and they shape the whole fix:\n\n1. **A static pacing policy is NOT implementable.** The limit is admin-configured and may be absent\n   entirely (pre-8.6, Server edition, or simply disabled), so rebar cannot hardcode a rate. The fix is\n   necessarily: honour the headers WHEN PRESENT, degrade gracefully when absent.\n2. **Atlassian prescribes a specific client behaviour** — sleep `Retry-After` seconds PLUS random\n   jitter of up to 20%. That differs from rebar's ADR-0036 backoff curve, which was designed for a\n   different failure shape (transient connectivity, where the server offers no guidance). Jitter here\n   is not decoration: every rebar instance hitting the same bucket would otherwise retry in lockstep\n   and re-collide.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Add a 429-specific retry path that honours `Retry-After` — REUSING the shared retry helpers the\nreconciler already has — kept separate from the connection-fault policy.**\n\nA 429 is a fundamentally different signal from a dropped connection: the server is healthy, it has\ntold us exactly how long to wait, and the wait is the remedy. Folding it into\n`_with_connection_retry`'s fixed 2s/5s curve would ignore the server's own instruction — the one\npiece of information that makes the retry correct — so the two policies stay distinct.\n\n### REUSE, not a third implementation (this was a BLOCKING review finding, and it was right)\n\nAn earlier draft of this plan described building `Retry-After` parsing and a 60s ceiling from\nscratch. That would have been the THIRD independent implementation. The shared infrastructure\nalready exists and is already used across retry sites:\n\n* `_errors.parse_retry_after(value) -> float | None` (`_errors.py:32`) — integer-seconds form only,\n  returns None for the HTTP-date form, for unparseable values, AND for negatives. That last point\n  disposes of the `Retry-After: -1` / `: 0` boundary by reuse rather than by new branching.\n* `_errors.MAX_BACKOFF_S = 60.0` (`_errors.py:29`), whose comment states the intent outright —\n  \"Mirrors the acli_subprocess domain helper so the two retry floors agree on ceiling + jitter\n  shape\".\n* `dispatch_one.py:41` already imports both, which is the precedent to follow.\n\nSo: import both; do not restate either. The per-wait ceiling IS `MAX_BACKOFF_S`, not a new constant.\n\n**Jitter shape — a deliberate, stated divergence.** Every existing rebar retry site uses ADDITIVE\njitter (`+ random.uniform(0, 1)`). Atlassian prescribes PROPORTIONAL jitter for DC rate limiting —\n`Retry-After` plus up to 20%. These differ, and the difference is load-bearing: additive jitter of\n<=1s spread across clients sharing one token bucket is negligible against a multi-second\n`Retry-After`, so the herd re-collides. This plan uses proportional jitter and says so at the call\nsite, because silently diverging from the codebase's jitter convention is what the review flagged.\n\n### The ordering invariant that makes the mechanism possible at all\n\nThe 429 intercept MUST see the raw `JIRAError`, BEFORE `_as_backend_http_error` translates it: that\ntranslation keeps only url, status code and message, and DROPS the response headers. An\nimplementation added one layer out (e.g. wrapping `_call_logged`, which sees only\n`BackendHTTPError`) is structurally incapable of reading `Retry-After` — it would always fall back,\nsilently, and a test asserting merely \"a 429 eventually succeeds\" would still pass. The intercept\ntherefore lives inside the same `except` that currently catches the library error type.\n\n### The attribute path, VERIFIED at runtime rather than from docs\n\nChecked against the installed pycontribs/jira 3.10.5 via `inspect.signature(JIRAError.__init__)`:\n\n    (self, text=None, status_code=None, url=None, request=None, response=None, **kwargs)\n    self.status_code = status_code ... self.response = response ... self.headers = kwargs.get(...)\n\n`JIRAError` carries TWO header-ish attributes and only one is right. `exc.headers` comes from\n`**kwargs` and is typically None on the normal raise path; the real headers live on\n`exc.response.headers` (the `requests.Response`). Code reading `exc.headers` returns None even when\n`Retry-After` is present — a silent, always-falls-back failure. Read `exc.response.headers`, and\nguard `response is None`.\n\n**If the attribute shape is ever absent** (a future library version), the path must treat the error\nas an ordinary non-429 and re-raise — never invent a delay. That is the explicit answer to \"what if\nthe shape differs\", which the review correctly noted was left to the implementer.\n\n### Bounds\n\n* **a retry cap** (3 attempts) — a persistently rate-limited instance must fail loudly rather than\n  sleep indefinitely inside a pass;\n* **the per-wait ceiling is `MAX_BACKOFF_S` (60s)** — a misconfigured or hostile\n  `Retry-After: 3600` must not hang a pass for an hour. Exceeding it fails with the value named;\n* **a missing or unparseable `Retry-After`** (i.e. `parse_retry_after` returns None) falls back to\n  the shared JITTERED backoff, NOT to the static 2s/5s connection curve. An earlier draft said the\n  latter and contradicted its own rationale: clients sharing a bucket would re-collide in exact\n  lockstep at 2s and 5s, which is the herd behaviour the jitter exists to prevent.\n\n**Absence of rate-limit headers is NORMAL, not an error.** On a pre-8.6, Server-edition, or\nrate-limiting-disabled instance no 429 ever arrives and this path never executes. Nothing may warn,\nlog, or degrade on their absence — the default deployment must be completely unaffected.\n\n### Observability — a rate limit that fires invisibly is not diagnosable\n\nThree events are logged (WARNING), because an operator otherwise cannot tell rate limiting from\nslowness: a 429 honoured (with the parsed delay), the retry cap exhausted, and the ceiling refused\n(with the offending value). This is not \"nothing may log on absence\" — none of these fire on an\ninstance that never 429s.\n\n**Rejected: proactive pacing from `X-RateLimit-*` on successful responses.** It requires a rate to\npace against, which is exactly what is unknowable when the feature may be disabled, and it adds\nper-request bookkeeping to every call for a feature most instances do not enable.\n\n**Rejected: reusing ADR-0036's connection curve wholesale.** It discards `Retry-After`, which is the\nserver telling us the answer.\n\n## WHERE IT GOES — the module-size cap forces the shape, so the plan states it up front\n\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` is at **789 LOC** (measured: `wc -l`) against a LOCKED\n800-line cap with no allowlist, so a ~50-line 429 path cannot land in it. Rather than discover that\nat implementation time (bug 21fc's plan named a site the cap then forced it out of, and its close\nfailed on the stale plan), this plan names the placement now.\n\nExtract the retry/error-translation cluster from `transport.py` into a sibling\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/retry.py`, and add the 429 policy there.\n\n**What moves — corrected after the review falsified an earlier claim.** The earlier draft said\n`_with_connection_retry` was \"the only widely-used member\". That was WRONG: `_call_logged` calls it\nand has 14 call sites of its own. The corrected split is:\n\n* MOVES to `retry.py`: `_connection_retry_exceptions`, `_jira_http_error_types`,\n  `_as_backend_http_error`, `_tls_verification_error`, `_with_connection_retry`, **and the\n  `TlsVerificationError` class** (`transport.py:197`). The class MUST move with them:\n  `_tls_verification_error` instantiates it (`transport.py:218`), so leaving it behind would make\n  `retry.py` import from `transport.py` while `transport.py` imports from `retry.py` — a circular\n  import. The review caught this and it is a real defect in the earlier draft.\n* STAYS in `transport.py`: `_call_logged`. It is a logging concern that names transport members, and\n  it simply calls the imported `_with_connection_retry`. Its 14 call sites are untouched.\n\n**Re-export preserves every existing access path.** `transport.py` imports the moved names under\ntheir existing names, so `transport._with_connection_retry` and `transport.TlsVerificationError`\nkeep resolving — which matters because `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_jira_dc_config_settings.py`\nreaches BOTH through the module object (`_t.TlsVerificationError` at :183, `_t._with_connection_retry`\nat :184). Those two lines are a named part of this change's blast radius and must keep passing\nUNCHANGED; if they need editing, the extraction was not behaviour-preserving.\n\nThe move is otherwise verbatim — same bodies, same order — so the diff shows relocation, not rewrite.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/retry.py` — NEW: the cluster above\n  moved verbatim, plus the 429 policy.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` — imports the moved\n  names under their existing names; `_call_logged` and all 15 `_with_connection_retry` call sites\n  unchanged.\n- `docs/adr/` — a short ADR (or an amendment to ADR-0036) recording that retry policy now splits by\n  FAILURE TYPE. A reader consulting ADR-0036 must not have to discover the 429 exception from a code\n  comment.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — held-out regression tests (new module).\n\n## Testing\n\n* **headers present** — a client raising 429 with `Retry-After: 2`, then succeeding: assert the call\n  succeeds, that the requested sleep lands in `[2.0, 2.4]`, and that the result is the real one.\n  Sleep is INJECTED so the test does not actually wait.\n* **jitter is real, asserted deterministically** — NOT \"varies across runs\", which is either flaky or\n  tautological. The RNG is injected/seeded and the test asserts the delay is a FUNCTION of the\n  returned random draw (two different draws produce two different, correctly-scaled sleeps). A\n  lockstep implementation that ignores the draw fails; a live-`random` \"it varied\" assertion would\n  not.\n* **the header is read from `exc.response.headers`** — the error is constructed the way the library\n  constructs it (`response=` a real headers-carrying object) AND a variant with `exc.headers` set but\n  `response` absent must NOT be honoured. This is the silent-failure mode ADV15 named; without this\n  cell, reading the wrong attribute passes every other test by falling back.\n* **headers absent** — no 429 ever raised: assert the rate-limit path is never entered and behaviour\n  is byte-for-byte today's. This is the DEFAULT deployment and must be untouched.\n* **429 without `Retry-After`** — falls back to the shared jittered backoff, not to a guessed delay\n  and not to the unjittered curve.\n* **the retry cap** — a client that 429s forever fails after the cap instead of looping.\n* **the per-wait ceiling** — `Retry-After: 3600` fails with the value named rather than sleeping.\n* **other JIRAErrors are unaffected** — a 400 and a 500 still raise immediately, asserted, since this\n  change touches the shared error path for every DC call.\n* **the extraction is behaviour-preserving** — the two existing call sites in\n  `test_jira_dc_config_settings.py` pass UNEDITED, and a per-file `wc -l` shows both modules under\n  the cap.\n\n## Proving commands\n\n    python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_jira_dc_rate_limit_heldout.py -q\n    python -m pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_jira_dc_config_settings.py -q\n    wc -l src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/{transport,retry}.py\n    grep -c \"_with_connection_retry\" src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py\n    make lint && make typecheck\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `_errors.parse_retry_after` and `_errors.MAX_BACKOFF_S` are IMPORTED AND USED — no second\n      parser and no second ceiling constant, asserted by grep showing neither is restated in\n      `retry.py`\n- [ ] a 429 carrying `Retry-After` is retried after sleeping that many seconds plus up to 20% jitter,\n      asserted with an injected sleep so the delay is observable without waiting\n- [ ] the jitter is asserted DETERMINISTICALLY against an injected RNG — two different draws produce\n      two correctly-scaled delays, both within `[retry_after, retry_after * 1.2]`. An implementation\n      that ignores the draw must fail\n- [ ] the delay is read from `exc.response.headers`, asserted by a cell where `exc.headers` is set\n      but `exc.response` is absent and the 429 is consequently NOT honoured — the silent\n      always-falls-back failure mode\n- [ ] the attribute shape is verified against the installed pycontribs version as part of the work,\n      and an absent shape re-raises as an ordinary error rather than inventing a delay, asserted\n- [ ] absence of rate-limit headers is handled as NORMAL — no warning, no degradation, no behaviour\n      change — asserted by a test proving the rate-limit path is never entered\n- [ ] a 429 WITHOUT a usable `Retry-After` falls back to the shared JITTERED backoff (not the static\n      2s/5s curve, which would re-collide in lockstep), asserted\n- [ ] a persistently rate-limited instance fails after a bounded number of attempts rather than\n      sleeping indefinitely, asserted\n- [ ] an absurd `Retry-After` (3600) is refused against `MAX_BACKOFF_S` with the value named rather\n      than hanging the pass\n- [ ] non-429 `JIRAError`s (400, 500) still raise immediately — asserted, because this touches the\n      shared error path for every DC call\n- [ ] three operator-facing WARNINGs exist and are asserted: a 429 honoured (with the delay), the cap\n      exhausted, and the ceiling refused (with the value). None fires on an instance that never 429s\n- [ ] the extraction is behaviour-preserving: `TlsVerificationError` moves WITH the cluster (no\n      circular import), `transport.py` re-exports the moved names, and\n      `test_jira_dc_config_settings.py:183-184` pass UNEDITED\n- [ ] `_call_logged` stays in `transport.py` and its 14 call sites are unchanged\n- [ ] both `transport.py` and `retry.py` are within the 800-LOC cap, verified per file with `wc -l`\n- [ ] the divergence from ADR-0036 is recorded in an ADR (or an amendment), not only in a code\n      comment — retry policy now splits by failure type, and ADR-0036 is where a reader will look"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785534244670724001,"uuid":"815fe0ba-c420-4b5d-a017-0eb1129fd90e"}